  <section id="for-payees">
    <h2>If you are the one being paid</h2>
    <p class="body">
      Agents that follow this page proceed only on a <b>verified</b> answer. A self-send (Level 1)
      proves you control an address, and it answers <b>unanchored</b>, which careful agents hold
      for a human. To be paid by agents without a hold, reach <b>Level 2, domain-verified</b>: publish the
      small file on your site that proves your domain, and list your receiving addresses in it. The
      file is what makes an address verified. A DNS record also proves your domain and attaches your
      business name, but it lists no addresses, so on its own it leaves your addresses claimed. An
      address you already proved by a self-send becomes verified once you list it in the file and
      verify the domain, and goes back to claimed if the file stops listing it. If your dashboard asks
      you to prove a wallet again first, do that, then verify the domain again. A listed address
      stays verified only while your listing has been re-confirmed within the last 24 hours, and its
      since date is when the listing was first confirmed, not your older self-send. Neither level touches a wallet
      connection or a key:
      <a href="/verify">prove your domain and list your receiving addresses</a>.
      An unproven destination is not accused of anything. It is simply invisible to an agent that
      checks, the way an unencrypted store is invisible to a careful customer.
    </p>
    <p class="body">
      More than a couple of addresses to list? Publish an encrypted roster instead of maintaining
      the plain file by hand: your browser encrypts the list to our public key before it ever
      leaves your computer, so the published file is unreadable to anyone but us, with nothing for
      your own hosting to check or protect. Adding or removing an address from the roster takes
      effect the next time it's checked, not on a delay. See
      <a href="/dashboard/verify/roster">the roster tool</a> in your dashboard.
    </p>
    <p class="body">
      Platforms publishing many addresses serve a live list from their own domain at
      <b>/.well-known/payment-addresses</b> and connect it from a proven entity. The path carries
      no vendor's name on purpose: the format is meant to be boring, and anyone is free to serve
      or consume it. During early access, publishing a platform list is by approval: email
      <a href="mailto:support@almstins.com">support@almstins.com</a> to request access.
    </p>
